 {"id": "TJ-DF-038", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "complete", "title": "[P3] Missing `uname` on PATH is diagnosed as 'this CLI requires Linux PID namespaces' \u2014 a Linux host told it is not Linux", "description": "Verified 2026-09-27 by running the installed CLI with a deliberately crippled PATH: the wrapper's preflight does `if [ \"$(uname -s)\" != \"Linux\" ]` and, when `uname` is absent, the command substitution is empty, so the wrapper exits 2 printing 'terminal-jail: this CLI requires Linux PID namespaces' (plus a stray 'line 452: uname: command not found' from the shell). The operator is sent to investigate their kernel when the real problem is a stripped PATH (containers, minimal images, sandboxed exec wrappers). Found while reviewing; the bwrap-absent probe had to be rebuilt with a fuller PATH to get an uncontaminated result, which is exactly the confusion a user would hit.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The preflight distinguishes 'uname is not on PATH' from 'this is not Linux' and says so, naming the missing tool (or reads /proc/version as a fallback so the check cannot be defeated by a thin PATH). 2) Same treatment for the other preflight binaries it depends on, at minimum unshare (already named) \u2014 a missing dependency must never be reported as a platform limitation. 3) A test runs the CLI with a PATH containing only the minimum, asserting the diagnostic names the missing tool and that the exit code still fails closed.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0, "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "cd31f7b", "completed_at": "2026-09-29T10:20:00-05:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-29T10:20:00-05:00", "worker_summary": "Preflight: uname-absent falls back to /proc/sys/kernel/ostype then /proc/version before claiming non-Linux; if neither verifies, names uname as missing (install coreutils) and exits 2 fail-closed. 8 tests added (plugin/test_standalone_cli.py), full plugin suite 1454 passed / 7 skipped. Live probe PATH=/tmp/bare (no uname): help renders normally on this Linux host.", "guard_result": "Tier1 DEGRADED PASS (secrets+static_analysis clean; lint/tests skipped, nothing staged at post-commit guard)"}
 {"id": "JSONL-NORM-002", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Board schema drift: `priority` is not type-stable (P1 strings + bare ints) and 7 rows still use the legacy `completed` status next to 175 `complete`", "description": "Measured on this board 2026-09-27 (209 rows, last line per id wins): priority values are mixed types \u2014 72 \"P1\" strings alongside 9 bare 1 ints, likewise P2 (72 strings / 7 ints), P3 (35 strings), P0 (11 strings); status uses both `complete` (175) and the legacy synonym `completed` (7). Related: 183 of 209 rows have no `type` field at all while 25 set \"task\", and `created_by` is null on 176 rows. Any count, sort or filter written against these fields is one drifted row away from being wrong, and the project's own recurring work is exactly counting and filtering rows (PM/releng/QA lanes).", "acceptance_criteria": "1) `priority` is one type everywhere (string, normalized to P0-P3) and `status` is one vocabulary (`complete`), with `completed` migrated. 2) A guard/probe (extend scripts/board_id_guard.py or a sibling) FAILS on a type-inconsistent priority or an unknown status, so the drift cannot return. 3) The migration is applied to tasks.jsonl in a single pass, with the census (row count + unique ids) printed before and after and quoted in the closing commit. 4) Counts quoted elsewhere (README/board summaries) are re-derived after the migration.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-12", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Skip marker contradicts the host probe: a test skips as HOST-DEGRADED-PIDNS while scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py classifies this host FULL and the CLI demonstrably contains", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31: the suite reports 'SKIPPED [1] test_env_scrub.py:440 HOST-DEGRADED-PIDNS: host refused bare PID namespace creation', but scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py on the same host prints FULL (exit 0) and the live CLI creates a PID namespace on demand (bare mode: jail PID-ns inode 4026536818 vs host 4026531836, 5 visible PIDs vs 1129). So either the marker overstates a narrower precondition, or the classifier and the test disagree about what DEGRADED means. Both are bad in the same way: an honest-skip system that mislabels why it skipped is indistinguishable, in the output, from a real DEGRADED host \u2014 and this project's credibility rests on those skip names being true.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The skip marker names the actual failed precondition (and the probe it agrees with), or the test runs on this host. Whichever is correct, the test's own message and the classifier agree. 2) A genuine DEGRADED host still skips with a truthful marker: prove it by exercising the classifier's two branches (e.g. a forced-degraded env or a documented fixture) and quoting both outputs. 3) The suite's skip output is cross-checked in CI (or by a QA cell) against the classifiers so a mislabelled skip is detected, not discovered by review. 4) No coverage is silently dropped: if the skipped assertion IS runnable here, it runs.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
-{"id": "INT-CI-2", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] '1437 tests' is a count, not a coverage claim: no pytest-cov in the dev group, so CI cannot trend what the suite does not touch", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on the fresh clone: `uv sync --dev` installs pytest + ruff + pyyaml only \u2014 [dependency-groups] dev = [\"pytest>=8\", \"ruff==0.16.8\"] \u2014 and there is no coverage tooling anywhere in the install path, so the suite's size (1437 passed / 7 skipped in 46 s, verified) cannot be turned into a coverage number by anyone, including CI, whose jobs are test/lint/audit/drift-guards. For a security tool the untested branches are the interesting ones, and a project whose README leans on its test count should be able to say what those tests reach.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) pytest-cov is in the dev dependency group (no new runtime dependency). 2) A documented command produces a coverage number for plugin/terminal_jail, and the measured number is recorded in the repo (README development section and/or a CI artifact) with the command that produced it. 3) CI reports it (job or artifact) so it can trend, and a floor is set at or below the measured baseline so regressions are visible \u2014 the floor must be the measured value, not an aspirational target. 4) The honest caveats are stated where the number is published (what is excluded: tests themselves, platform-gated paths).", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
-{"id": "DOC-8", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Nothing states what containment a reader actually gets on their host: publish the per-host capability matrix with the probes that produce it", "description": "The README is unusually honest about individual limitations (private /proc is bubblewrap-only; the filesystem tier needs a uid mapping; the unshare path exposes host /proc) \u2014 the strongest trust signal in the project, and the review says so. But those limits are spread across prose, COMPATIBILITY.md and docs/backend-parity.md, so a reader cannot answer the one question that decides adoption: 'on MY host, with MY kernel and distro, which of the three layers actually enforce?' The measured 2026-09-27 answer for Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31 \u2014 PID namespace YES, identity scrub YES, seccomp YES (with --seccomp), private /proc only under bwrap, filesystem isolation NO (DEGRADED) \u2014 is exactly the kind of table the project should print, next to the two probes that generate it.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) One place (README section + docs) holds a capability matrix: rows = the enforcement layers (PID namespace, identity/env scrub, private /proc, filesystem isolation, seccomp, egress), columns = the backends and/or host classes, cells = enforced / degraded / unavailable with the reason. 2) The commands that produce the read for a given host (scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py, scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py) are named in the same place, so a reader can generate their own row. 3) The Ubuntu AppArmor case is stated in the matrix AND in the adoption guidance, with the remediation, so 'filesystem isolation: unavailable on Ubuntu 24.04+ by default' is not discoverable only by reading a warning at runtime. 4) Claims are pinned by tests/docs checks as the repo already does elsewhere (no drifting numbers).", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
+{"id": "INT-CI-2", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "complete", "title": "[P3] '1437 tests' is a count, not a coverage claim: no pytest-cov in the dev group, so CI cannot trend what the suite does not touch", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on the fresh clone: `uv sync --dev` installs pytest + ruff + pyyaml only — [dependency-groups] dev = [\"pytest>=8\", \"ruff==0.16.8\"] — and there is no coverage tooling anywhere in the install path, so the suite's size (1437 passed / 7 skipped in 46 s, verified) cannot be turned into a coverage number by anyone, including CI, whose jobs are test/lint/audit/drift-guards. For a security tool the untested branches are the interesting ones, and a project whose README leans on its test count should be able to say what those tests reach.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) pytest-cov is in the dev dependency group (no new runtime dependency). 2) A documented command produces a coverage number for plugin/terminal_jail, and the measured number is recorded in the repo (README development section and/or a CI artifact) with the command that produced it. 3) CI reports it (job or artifact) so it can trend, and a floor is set at or below the measured baseline so regressions are visible — the floor must be the measured value, not an aspirational target. 4) The honest caveats are stated where the number is published (what is excluded: tests themselves, platform-gated paths).", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0, "worker_status": "complete", "completed_at": "2026-10-04T04:23:48Z", "commit_hash": "442013a", "guard_result": "N/A (verification-only close, no new code)", "foreman_note": "Verification-only close, tick terminal-jail-foreman-2026-10-04-04-11-26. Fix already landed: b08d0ae (pytest-cov>=6 in dev group, measured baseline 75% plugin-scope) + 442013a (coverage.xml artifact upload). Both ancestors of HEAD (merge-base --is-ancestor OK). CI run on 0cddb49 success (gh run list 2026-10-04T00:22Z), coverage step + upload-artifact@v4 present in ci.yml at HEAD. No worker dispatched; gitreins lifecycle not re-run (zero-diff judge = phantom)."}
+{"id": "DOC-8", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Nothing states what containment a reader actually gets on their host: publish the per-host capability matrix with the probes that produce it", "description": "The README is unusually honest about individual limitations (private /proc is bubblewrap-only; the filesystem tier needs a uid mapping; the unshare path exposes host /proc) — the strongest trust signal in the project, and the review says so. But those limits are spread across prose, COMPATIBILITY.md and docs/backend-parity.md, so a reader cannot answer the one question that decides adoption: 'on MY host, with MY kernel and distro, which of the three layers actually enforce?' The measured 2026-09-27 answer for Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31 — PID namespace YES, identity scrub YES, seccomp YES (with --seccomp), private /proc only under bwrap, filesystem isolation NO (DEGRADED) — is exactly the kind of table the project should print, next to the two probes that generate it.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) One place (README section + docs) holds a capability matrix: rows = the enforcement layers (PID namespace, identity/env scrub, private /proc, filesystem isolation, seccomp, egress), columns = the backends and/or host classes, cells = enforced / degraded / unavailable with the reason. 2) The commands that produce the read for a given host (scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py, scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py) are named in the same place, so a reader can generate their own row. 3) The Ubuntu AppArmor case is stated in the matrix AND in the adoption guidance, with the remediation, so 'filesystem isolation: unavailable on Ubuntu 24.04+ by default' is not discoverable only by reading a warning at runtime. 4) Claims are pinned by tests/docs checks as the repo already does elsewhere (no drifting numbers).", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0, "worker_status": "in_progress", "foreman_note": "Wave worker dispatched tick terminal-jail-foreman-2026-10-04-04-11-26 in worktree /home/kara/worktrees/terminal-jail-DOC-8 (branch wt/DOC-8, base 0cddb49)."}
